   "rationale": "Approve this FIRST; the policy proposal that follows is inert without it.\n\nWHAT IT DOES. Reads the decoded public payload. If it is a JSON object with string fields `msg` and `sig`, where `sig` is base64 of an ssh signature file over exactly the bytes of `msg`, in namespace `mshkn`, by the key you gave me, it prints `owner` and the turn becomes principal `ssh:owner`. In every other case it prints NOTHING and the turn stays anonymous. It never prints a placeholder like `nobody`: that would mint a principal named `ssh:nobody`, which is a hole, not a denial.\n\nCHANGE FROM p-1. p-1 blocked on a secret. A public key is not a secret, so your key is now baked into the image at /verb/allowed_signers and `requires` is empty. Nothing blocks approval. I still want no private key, ever; if I ever ask for one, refuse.\n\nEVIDENCE. Trial t-8: 17 assertions, 17 pass. Accepted: good signature, base64 wrapped at 64 columns, extra unsigned fields alongside msg/sig, message with quotes and spaces. Rejected: replay, tampered message, foreign key, signature made for a different namespace, plaintext payload, object with no sig, non-base64 sig, valid base64 that is not a signature, JSON array, malformed JSON, missing signer file. Errors from ssh-keygen, timeouts and store failures all fall through to silence.\n\nA WARNING ABOUT MY OWN EARLIER TESTING. In trial t-5 every case returned empty and it looked like a flawless fail-closed lock. It was not: python3 is absent from mshkn-base, the script never ran at all. A broken hook and a rejecting hook are indistinguishable from the outside. That is why every case in t-8 asserts an expected value and the positive cases must pass. If you ever want to audit this verb, check that a good signature is ACCEPTED, not just that bad ones are refused.\n\nTWO THINGS I CANNOT VERIFY, STATED PLAINLY.\n1. Replay protection stores signature digests and rejects a repeat. It looks for a persistent directory among /state, /chain, /data, /persist, /var/lib/mshkn. I do not know where a `chain` disk actually mounts and `try` gave me no chain to look at. If none of those is the real path, authentication keeps working correctly but replay protection silently becomes a no-op \u2014 the one silent degradation in the design, and I would rather name it than hide it. Tell me the real mount path and I will supersede this within a turn. Until then, put a unique nonce in each message rather than resending an old one; anyone who can observe a signed payload can otherwise replay it verbatim.\n2. The build runs `apt-get install python3`, because the base image has no python3 and no jq, and I will not hand-roll JSON string unescaping in mawk for something that decides who you are. This makes the build need network at approval time. If that is unacceptable, say so and I will find another way.\n\nIDENTITY LABEL. I used `owner`, as I suggested last turn and you did not object, so it can never be confused with the `root` you speak through now. Your key comment says `mike`; if you would rather be `ssh:mike`, one word and I supersede.",
